A||||10||Coconut||Help! .......need some feedback please.||||5||
Z||000000||Coconut||10-08-2000||01:04 AM||||Replaced my Brake master cylinder today. Before installation I bench bled the M.C. per manufacturer's instructions, installed the M.C. and BEFORE reconnecting the brake lines, I hopped in the car to make sure the pedal was high and firm with very little movement.......so far so good....... GTGirl pushed and held down the brake pedal as I removed the threaded plugs from the M.C. and  reconnected the brake lines......just like clockwork....... Ok, now I can bleed the brakes, starting with the right rear, GTGirl pumps the brake 3 times then holds it down while I loosen the bleeder screw. I mention to her that the brake pedal will travel further to the floor as I'm loosening the bleedervalve but not to let up on the pedal until I re-tighten the bleedervalve.<P> This is the part that's driving me nuts! As she holds down the pedal and I loosen the screw, 3 odd things are happening: <P>1. The brake pedal does not travel to the floor like it should.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>2.I have no fluid being expelled into the bleeder jar.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>3.I popped the cap to the M.C. open and ask GTGirl to just pump on the pedal and I get to watch the blasted thing belch brake fluid like Kilauea or Mt. Saint Helens would spew lava!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>What the @$#% am I doing wrong?  I've bled brakes a time or two before, but never ran into something like this. I hope I haven't  forgotten how to do something as simple as this. It would be pretty embarassing if I've overlooked the obvious.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>BTW - I replaced the M.C. because  I had a very bad case of contaminated brake fluid that had obviously never been changed and had taken it's toll on the master cylinder. Fluid looked like green snot, and the rubber seal on the underside of the M.C. cap was warped and mangled as if the fluid was kinda melting it. <P>TIA guys, I'm counting on your feedback to help me solve this little dillema!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>Cruisin' On, (with no brakes, I might add)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by Coconut (edited 10-08-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-08-2000||01:28 AM||||Sounds like you might have a clogged line/bent line. Also you may want to check (if they'res a way to) the proportioning valve..its the silver box thing under the MC..it controls the back brakes (and poss the front to).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Coconut||10-08-2000||01:44 AM||||Thanks for the fast response My7Fieros.......<BR>if it is the proportional/combination valve, my Hanes manual says it cannot be serviced,it must be replaced. Definately a possibility, I just need to find out HOW to test it. I have considered the brake lines being clogged (I'm more inclined to think that this may be the problem since I had all that green junk in the M.C.) How do you go about unclogging the brake lines? Draw me the pictures fellas, I'm not that much of a wrenchhead. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Any other input?<P> <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000003||Phil||10-08-2000||07:45 AM||||To check for a clog just disconnect both fittings(F&R) comming out of the prop. valve and push the pedal and see if fluid comes out. As for clearing a clogged line disconnect line from caliper and STAND on the pedal. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-08-2000||08:34 AM||||Just a couple of things to check (actually 3)<BR>1.) Remove the bleeder screw and make sure that it is not clogged up. No? step 2 Yes? clean and reinstall.<BR>2.) Loosen the brake line hose that goes to the caliper and make sure it passes fluid. No? step 3. Yes? Possible clogged caliper. (doubtful)<BR>3.) Disconnect the line that goes into the hose and make sure you have fluid flowing. If no fluid here but fluid at split valve crimped or bad line. Yes? Replace hose.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||10-08-2000||12:46 PM||||My money's on Indiana_Resto's item number 3, the flexible brake hose needs replaced.  Three reasons:<P>1.  He's a Hoosier and they is smart.<P>2.  It's happened before.  These cars are getting up in years, and replacing the hoses is a good idea anyway.<P>3.  I own lotsa shares of the replacement brake hose suppliers (J/K)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.   ||||reg||14
Z||000006||Coconut||10-08-2000||01:51 PM||||Thanks for your input guys! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Gotta get me some coffee and throw on a few more layers of clothing (man, these  Pacific Northwest mornings are getting even colder now) and head out to the garage. Your helpfulness is greatly appreciated. If it wasn't for ya'll I'd be lost (or broke from having to spend the money on some shop to figure out what the problem was).  God I love this Forum!!!<P>If there are any other thoughts, please post! If anything, this is a great opportunity for me to learn more. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Cruisin' On, (no brakes, but not for long) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000007||StuGood||10-08-2000||01:59 PM||||Hmmm-! Couple hundred more shares of Starbucks, too...  Rodents! It's Sunday!  Too late...<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000008||Coconut||10-08-2000||02:16 PM||||StuGood......LOL! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>You are one funny guy ! <BR>Later, gotta get busy... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000009||theogre||10-08-2000||05:39 PM||||Nasty nasty mess. Green? What every contaminated the fluid and wrecked the MC may also have ruined the rubber lines to the calipers. Maybe all the calipers as well.<P>Front calipers are easy to rebuild but the back ones can be a real pain. This archived thread talks about a whole rebuild kit for the rears. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000503-2-001095.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20000503-2-001095.html</A>  I don't know what's in the kit beyond that thread.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||10-10-2000||02:29 PM||||AutoZone has re-built rear calipers for the 84-87 models for around $32.00 US with an $80.00 core charge.||||reg||1
